:: StoryTitle {"position":"0,700"}
Cam Safety

:: StoryInit
/* Content macros: each records one stage slot's content (src/js/stage.js,
   which writes the slots once per passage), and renders nothing into
   #passages, so the stage owns all visible content for [checker] passages.

   Authoring:
     <<set_layout 2>>
     <<cam_line "Cam's line">>
     <<player_line "The player's reply">>
     <<portrait "images/cam/phase-3/talking/phase-3-talking.gif" "alt (opt)">>
   <<set_layout>> chooses this passage's layout directly: it publishes the
   number (0-3) while the passage renders, before the passage is displayed,
   so the first visible frame is already composed. It renders nothing.
   <<cam_line>> is spoken: the stage reveals it one character at a time, with a
   letter of voice per character (src/js/reveal.js, src/js/voice.js).
   <<player_line>> is the player's own line and is not: it arrives whole, once
   Cam has finished, and its links are silent and never half-revealed. Neither
   line is revealed under prefers-reduced-motion.
   An omitted <<portrait>> leaves the layout's neutral pose in place (the
   NEUTRAL_POSE table in src/js/stage.js), so most passages need no portrait
   line at all. The lines are wiki-processed, so $variables, markup and [[links]] work in
   them. A [checker] passage's exits belong in <<player_line>> (the reply
   bar — or <<cam_line>>, the speech-bubble), never as a bare [[...]] in
   passage text: stage mode hides #passages, so a bare link is invisible to
   the player. */
<<script>>
(function () {
	Macro.add("set_layout", {
		handler : function () {
			if (this.args.length === 0) {
				return this.error("missing layout number (0-3)");
			}
			Layout.set(this.args[0]);
		}
	});

	Macro.add("cam_line", {
		handler : function () {
			Stage.setCamLine(this.args[0]);
		}
	});

	Macro.add("player_line", {
		handler : function () {
			Stage.setPlayerLine(this.args[0]);
		}
	});

	Macro.add("portrait", {
		handler : function () {
			Stage.setPortrait(this.args[0], this.args[1]);
		}
	});
})();
<</script>>

/* Cam's portrait needs no story variable: a [checker] passage that omits
   <<portrait>> shows the current layout's neutral pose (NEUTRAL_POSE in
   src/js/stage.js), and the phase GIFs live in include/images/cam/. */

:: Layouts [scratch] {"position":"0,750"}
/* Entry point for the four layout scratch passages — open it from the browser
   console:
     SugarCube.Engine.play("Layouts")
   Each link only navigates: the target passage's own <<set_layout>> applies
   while that passage renders, before it is displayed, so the first visible
   frame is already the layout it names. */
[[Layout 0->Layout 0]] · [[Layout 1->Layout 1]] · [[Layout 2->Layout 2]] · [[Layout 3->Layout 3]]

:: Layout 0 [checker scratch] {"position":"100,750"}
/* Wide and distant Cam (bottom-right, the default phase-0 neutral pose);
   bubble tail bottom-right; menu on. */
<<set_layout 0>>
<<cam_line "Layout 0 — wide and distant. The tail should point down-right at me.">>
<<player_line "Layout 0 reply. [[Layouts->Layouts]]">>

:: Layout 1 [checker scratch] {"position":"200,750"}
/* Cam moves to the bottom-left (the default phase-1 neutral pose); tail
   bottom-left; reply bar shifts right. */
<<set_layout 1>>
<<cam_line "Layout 1 — I am at the bottom-left now. The tail should point down-left at me.">>
<<player_line "Layout 1 reply. [[Layouts->Layouts]]">>

:: Layout 2 [checker scratch] {"position":"300,750"}
/* Cam moves to the upper-left (the default phase-2 neutral pose); tail
   top-left; reply bar drops slightly. */
<<set_layout 2>>
<<cam_line "Layout 2 — I am up in the top-left. The tail should point up-left at me.">>
<<player_line "Layout 2 reply. [[Layouts->Layouts]]">>

:: Layout 3 [checker scratch] {"position":"400,750"}
/* Tall close-in on the left (the default phase-3 neutral pose); tail
   left-mid; the menu is gone. */
<<set_layout 3>>
<<cam_line "Layout 3 — right up close. The tail should point left at me, and the menu should be gone.">>
<<player_line "Layout 3 reply. [[Layouts->Layouts]]">>

:: Stage content [checker scratch] {"position":"500,750"}
/* Bubble and reply bar filled; the portrait slot shows the layout's default
   neutral pose because this passage omits <<portrait>> — add one to override
   it for a single passage. The next-step link is rendered by <<player_line>>
   into the reply bar, because a [checker] passage's own #passages content is
   hidden in stage mode; reach this passage from the browser console:
     SugarCube.Engine.play("Stage content") */
<<cam_line "You again. At this hour.">>
<<player_line "The flock needed a night watch. [[Next (no macros)->Stage content empty]]">>

:: Stage content long [checker scratch] {"position":"550,750"}
/* A Cam line long enough to scroll the bubble. Scroll it and confirm the
   paper stays put and no line lands on its outline; the link inside the Cam
   line proves links stay bound and clickable after the reveal grows the
   prose. Reach it from the browser console:
     SugarCube.Engine.play("Stage content long") */
<<cam_line "This line is deliberately longer than the bubble, so the prose has to scroll inside it while the paper stays exactly where it is. Watch the card's edge: it should not move at all. The text should stop at the black outline rather than paint over it, and the paper's translucency and shadow should not fade a single letter. Keep reading, because the point is to see the same card hold still while the words travel. A link lives in this line too, [[Layouts->Layouts]], and once the line has landed it should still be a link you can click. That proves the reveal grew the text nodes in place instead of replacing the markup.">>
<<player_line "The long line has landed. [[Layouts->Layouts]]">>

:: Stage content empty [checker scratch] {"position":"600,750"}
/* No content macros: the bubble and reply slots must be empty — nothing left
   over from the previous passage — and the cam slot must show the layout's
   neutral pose, not a portrait the previous passage overrode. Use the
   browser's Back button to return. */